\par ```markdown\line # Skill: Evidence Quality for Clinical Efficacy Sections\line \line ## When to Apply\line Every generated Clinical Efficacy section.\line \line ## The Problem This Solves\line Generic drug efficacy claims without proper grading lead to:
\line - Regulatory criticism (insufficient evidence for claims)\line - HCP skepticism (unsupported statements)\line - Legal exposure (overstated benefits)\line \line This skill ensures all efficacy claims are evidence-graded.\line \line 
## Rules with Examples\line \line ### Rule 1: Evidence Grading Hierarchy\line For EVERY efficacy claim, assign one of these grades:\line \line \\*\\*Level 1A - Highest Quality\\*\\* (Randomized Controlled Trials, Meta-analyses)\line ```
\par Example GOOD claim:\line "A meta-analysis of 12 RCTs (N=3,400) showed efficacy in reducing\line HbA1c by 1.2% (95% CI: 1.0-1.4%) compared to placebo [Grade 1A]."
\par Example BAD claim:\line "Highly effective in controlling blood sugar."  \u8592\'3f No grade, no numbers
\par ```\line \line \\*\\*Level 1B\\*\\* (Large RCTs, non-inferiority studies)\line ```
\par Example GOOD:\line "In the landmark PIONEER trial (N=1,200 RCT), patients achieved\line HbA1c reduction of 1.5% [Grade 1B]."
\par ```\line \line \\*\\*Level 2\\*\\* (Observational, cohort studies)\line ```
\par Example GOOD:\line "Observational data (N=500 patients over 5 years) showed sustained\line blood glucose control [Grade 2]."
\par Example BAD:\line Using Level 2 evidence for PRIMARY efficacy claim (must be Level 1)
\par ```\line \line \\*\\*Level 3\\*\\* (Case reports, mechanistic studies, expert opinion)\line ```
\par Example GOOD:\line "In case reports, patients with renal impairment showed enhanced efficacy [Grade 3]."
\par ```\line \line ### Rule 2: Numeric Specificity\line EVERY efficacy claim must include specific numbers or percentage changes:\line \line ```
\par BAD:\line "The drug is effective in treating diabetes."
\par GOOD:\line "In a 12-week RCT, HbA1c reduction was 1.2 \'b1 0.3% (p<0.001)."
\par ```\line \line Metrics to extract from each study:\line - Effect size (absolute % change or relative risk reduction)\line - Confidence intervals (95% CI)\line - P-value or statistical significance\line - Sample size \\& duration\line 
