\par ```markdown\line # Skill: Pharmacology Section Quality Checks\line \line ## Overview\line This skill ensures Pharmacology sections meet organizational standards for depth, clarity, and evidence quality.\line \line ## When to Apply\line 
Apply to every generated Pharmacology section during generation phase.\line \line ## Instructions\line \line ### Rule 1: Mechanism Clarity\line The mechanism of action should be explained at 2 levels:\line - \\*\\*Molecular Level:\\*\\
* Specific receptor/enzyme interaction\line - \\*\\*Cellular/Physiological Level:\\*\\* How this translates to therapeutic effect\line \line Example Good Format:\line "Drug X is a potent inhibitor of enzyme Y (IC50 = 2 nM), preventing \line 
the conversion of substrate A to metabolite B, thereby increasing \line intracellular concentration of factor C, which activates pathway D \line leading to improved cellular function."\line \line Example Poor Format:\line 
"Drug X works by inhibiting enzyme Y to treat condition Z."\line \line ### Rule 2: Comparative Context\line If literature exists on similar drugs, include comparative statements:\line - "Unlike Drug A which inhibits X, Drug B inhibits both X and Y"\line 
- Quantify differences (potency, selectivity ratios)\line \line ### Rule 3: Evidence Grading\line For each major claim, assign evidence level:\line - \\*\\*Level 1:\\*\\* Randomized controlled trials, meta-analyses\line - \\*\\*Level 2:\\*\\
* Cohort studies, case-control studies\line - \\*\\*Level 3:\\*\\* Case reports, mechanistic studies\line - \\*\\*Level 4:\\*\\* Expert opinion\line \line 
Only use Level 1-2 evidence in Pharmacology section; cite supporting Level 3-4 as "supporting evidence"\line \line ## Quality Metrics\line - Mechanism explanation length: 100-300 words\line - Minimum 2 evidence sources per major claim\line 
